<p>
Suppose that $i$, $j$, $k$, and $l$ are four $(-2)$-indices with $a_{ij} > 0$, $a_{jk} > 0$, and $a_{kl} > 0$. In other words, the index $i$ “meets” $j$, $j$ “meets” $k$, and $k$ “meets” $l$. Then we see from Lemma <a href="/tag/0C7R" data-tag="0C7R">55.5.2</a> that $a_{ik} = a_{jl} = 0$. Since the matrix $A = (a_{ij})$ is semi-negative definite we see that the matrix 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \left( \begin{matrix}  -2w_ i 

&  a_{ij} 

&  0 

&  a_{il} 

\\  a_{ij} 

&  -2w_ j 

&  a_{jk} 

&  0 

\\  0 

&  a_{jk} 

